WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:03.213
- Training for sports gets you

00:00:03.213 --> 00:00:05.662
in a mindset of being perfect,

00:00:05.662 --> 00:00:08.609
of being exact, of being disciplined

00:00:08.609 --> 00:00:11.526
that carries over exactly to space.

00:00:14.324 --> 00:00:16.782
I was drafted to play out of college

00:00:16.782 --> 00:00:19.214
to the Detroit Lions in 1986.

00:00:19.214 --> 00:00:22.322
And then ended up playing
with the Dallas Cowboys

00:00:22.322 --> 00:00:24.243
as a wide receiver on both teams.

00:00:24.243 --> 00:00:25.304
And then I was injured.

00:00:25.304 --> 00:00:27.839
And it wasn't until after I left the NFL

00:00:27.839 --> 00:00:29.801
that I was interested in
becoming an astronaut.

00:00:29.801 --> 00:00:33.036
When you're launching
from Kennedy Space Center

00:00:33.036 --> 00:00:35.040
on millions of gallons of fuel,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.942
you are on the most incredible
rocket ride to space.

00:00:37.942 --> 00:00:39.168
And, when you get there,

00:00:39.168 --> 00:00:41.864
that adrenaline is still
raging through your body

00:00:41.864 --> 00:00:43.130
and you have to calm down a little bit.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:44.765
So, my training as an athlete

00:00:44.765 --> 00:00:47.706
helped me control that adrenaline rush.

00:00:47.706 --> 00:00:50.399
It made me focus and stay disciplined.

00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:52.567
The first time floating in space,

00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:55.508
you're trying to navigate
what you're doing

00:00:55.508 --> 00:00:57.099
and where you're going with your body.

00:00:57.099 --> 00:01:00.534
But training as an athlete
has you understanding

00:01:00.534 --> 00:01:02.209
a lot more about your body.

00:01:02.209 --> 00:01:04.743
And you're controlling these
little micro-accelerations

00:01:04.743 --> 00:01:06.906
with your body as you push off of things

00:01:06.906 --> 00:01:09.556
to make sure that you
don't damage anything.

00:01:09.556 --> 00:01:10.908
When I decided to become an astronaut,

00:01:10.908 --> 00:01:13.807
I saw it was very similar
to being an NFL player

00:01:13.807 --> 00:01:15.685
because there was teamwork involved.

00:01:15.685 --> 00:01:17.525
The quarterback is like the commander.

00:01:17.525 --> 00:01:19.118
So, the quarterback sets the tone,

00:01:19.118 --> 00:01:20.344
says "We're running this play.

00:01:20.344 --> 00:01:22.062
Leland, you do the down and out.

00:01:22.062 --> 00:01:24.638
And then I'm gonna throw you the ball."

00:01:24.638 --> 00:01:25.903
The commander on the mission says,

00:01:25.903 --> 00:01:27.866
"Leland, it's time for you
to take over this role.

00:01:27.866 --> 00:01:29.583
You know, we have a malfunction.

00:01:29.583 --> 00:01:31.502
I need you to do this."

00:01:31.502 --> 00:01:33.221
On the football field, you have to

00:01:33.221 --> 00:01:35.304
cowboy it up to get the win.

00:01:35.304 --> 00:01:36.572
The win in space is when you get

00:01:36.572 --> 00:01:37.837
your primary objectives done,

00:01:37.837 --> 00:01:39.594
like getting the Columbus Laboratory

00:01:39.594 --> 00:01:41.189
installed on the space station.

00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:42.660
And the real win is when you get home

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.577
and land and you're safe and sound.

